* ======================================================================
* secure.account.dir  -  lock this account's directory to its own group
*
* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - PROJECT_STATUS.md section 8, "the B work".  The data
*   tree grants sdusers Modify with (OI)(CI), so without this every account
*   directory inherits it and ANY SD user can read and rewrite ANY other
*   account's files outside SD.  Measured on the 16:54:55 install before this
*   went in: from an ordinary session, listing another account's directory
*   returned 6 entries and writing into it was allowed.
*
*   SD ALREADY REFUSES THAT SESSION, which is what makes this alignment rather
*   than new policy: logto.authorised admits an elevated session, one that has
*   just obtained privilege, or a member of the account's ACC$GROUP, and an
*   ordinary administrator is none of those - measured, "User not allowed in
*   requested account".  This brings the file layer into line with the rule
*   CPROC has enforced since 14 Aug 2026.
*
*   THE GROUP COMES FROM ACC$GROUP, the field written just above, so there is
*   no second mapping to keep in step.  An EMPTY group is skipped: locking a
*   directory to a group nobody can be in would take the account away from its
*   own user, and that is the one failure this must not have.
*
*   THROUGH !ps_script, like OS_GROUP's writing actions, so an unelevated SDSYS
*   session can perform it - section 7 step 4.
*
*   THE RULE IS ALSO IN gplbld/secure-account-dirs.ps1, which is the re-apply
*   half the installer runs for accounts that already exist.  TWO COPIES, and
*   the guard against them drifting is a measurement rather than a shared file:
*   gplbld/verify-accountacl.ps1 asserts that a directory this makes and one
*   that script stamps come out with the SAME ACL.
secure.account.dir:
   sad.group = trim(acc.rec<ACC$GROUP>)
   if sad.group = '' then return

*   ONE LINE WITH ";" SEPARATORS AND AN ELEVATION SELF-CHECK, which is
*   OS_GROUP's shape rather than an invention.  The helper runs elevated; if
*   this ever ran anywhere else the icacls would fail confusingly, so it says
*   exit 5 instead.
   sad.ps = 'if (-not (New-Object Security.Principal.WindowsPrincipal('
   sad.ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent())).IsInRole('
   sad.ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsBuiltInRole]::Administrator)) { exit 5 }; '
   sad.ps := '& icacls.exe "' : pathname : '" /inheritance:r'
   sad.ps := ' /grant "*S-1-5-18:(OI)(CI)(F)"'
   sad.ps := ' /grant "*S-1-5-32-544:(OI)(CI)(F)"'
   sad.ps := ' /grant "' : sad.group : ':(OI)(CI)(M)" | Out-Null; '
   sad.ps := 'exit $LASTEXITCODE'

   sad.rc = ps_script(sad.ps)
   if sad.rc # 0 then
      display sysmsg(10055, pathname, sad.rc) ;* Could not secure %1 (%2)
   end

   return

* ======================================================================
* grant.os.access  -  write this account's OS.USERS record, if it is to have one
*
* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - the ruling is at the call site.  os.sh and os.exec
* are set by "sh-on" and "os-on" in more.args and BOTH are set for an
* ADMINISTRATOR, so this routine does not test the tier itself: what it needs
* to know is already in the two flags, and asking twice is how two answers
* start to disagree.
*
* IT WRITES BOTH FIELDS EVEN WHEN ONLY ONE WAS ASKED FOR, "no" in the other.
* A record with one field is a record whose second field a later reader has to
* guess at, and both gates read them positionally.
*
* USER ACCOUNTS ONLY.  A GROUP account has no single person behind it and
* acc.uname is never assigned on that arm.
*
* AN EXISTING RECORD IS LEFT EXACTLY AS IT IS.  A "no" already in the file is
* somebody's decision and a create-time default has no business overruling it.
* It says so out loud rather than passing in silence, because "the
* administrator still cannot use the shell" is otherwise a question with
* nothing to read.
* ======================================================================

grant.os.access:
   if not(os.sh) and not(os.exec) then return
   if upcase(acc.type) # 'USER' then return
   if trim(acc.uname, ' ', 'B') = '' then return

   openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'os.users' to osu.f else
      display sysmsg(10104, acc.uname)
      * Unable to change operating-system access for %1
      return
   end

   read osu.rec from osu.f, acc.uname then
      close osu.f
      display sysmsg(10103, acc.uname)
      * %1 already has an os.users record, so it was left exactly as it was
      return
   end

   osu.sh = 'no'
   osu.ex = 'no'
   if os.sh   then osu.sh = 'yes'
   if os.exec then osu.ex = 'yes'
   osu.rec    = osu.sh        ;* field 1 - SH
   osu.rec<2> = osu.ex        ;* field 2 - OS.EXECUTE

   osu.written = @true
   write osu.rec to osu.f, acc.uname on error osu.written = @false
   close osu.f

   if osu.written then
      display sysmsg(10102, acc.uname, osu.sh, osu.ex)
      * os.users: %1 has SH %2, OS.EXECUTE %3
   end else
      display sysmsg(10104, acc.uname)
   end

   return

